<header>
  <h1>The Architecture of Curiosity</h1>
  <div class="meta">By Jane Doe · April 2026 · 8 min read</div>
</header>

<p>Curiosity is not a personality trait. It is a <em>scaffolding</em>: a structure we build inside ourselves that determines what questions we are even able to ask.</p>

<blockquote>"The art of being wise is the art of knowing what to overlook." — William James</blockquote>

<h2>Three pillars</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Surface area.</strong> Exposure to varied inputs.</li>
  <li><strong>Tolerance for ambiguity.</strong> Living with unresolved questions.</li>
  <li><strong>Time.</strong> The slow burn of follow-through.</li>
</ol>

<figure>
  <svg width="320" height="120" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
